      "text_raw": "For biasing, or DC setting\nof the currents, it may\nbe easier to introduce a\npMOST as the second or\nthird stage or even as a\nsource follower. In the\nexample in this slide, it is the\nthird stage which is using a\npMOST amplifier.\nObviously the expressions\nfor input and output imped-\nance do not change. The\nbiasing is easier however.\nIndeed the DC input volt-\nage is about 1 V above\nground, and so is the DC\noutput voltage. The other DC voltages are now easily found, depending on the transistor sizes\nand V values chosen.\nGS",
      "text": "For biasing, or DC setting of the currents, it may be easier to introduce a pMOST as the second or third stage or even as a source follower. In the example in this slide, it is the third stage which is using a pMOST amplifier. Obviously the expressions for input and output impedance do not change. The biasing is easier however. Indeed the DC input voltage is about 1 V above ground, and so is the DC output voltage. The other DC voltages are now easily found, depending on the transistor sizes and V values chosen. GS"
    }
